Subject: [PATCH] Blu-ray: let disc menus be brightened and saturated over HDR

Over HDR video a disc menu goes through the GUI composite: an 8-bit sRGB
GUI framebuffer that one PQ curve maps to 203 nit reference white. Next
to a bright film that menu can look dim and washed out, and nothing in
the composite can lift it - the GUI framebuffer already tops out at its
reference white.

Add "Disc menu brightness (HDR)" and "Disc menu saturation (HDR)" to the
Blu-ray disc settings. While either is off 100%, and only while Kodi's
own HDR composite is the PQ destination, HDMV and BD-J menu bitmaps take
the existing PQ bitmap route (RenderHDROverlays, straight into the PQ
back buffer) instead of the GUI composite. CB1's Dolby Vision and
rendering circuits are left alone: they compose PQ bitmaps into their
own picture, and a BD-J plane present for the whole title would change
how every frame is prepared there. The noblend shader then converts each
menu texel once: IEC 61966-2-1 decode and BT.709 -> BT.2020 exactly as
the GUI composite does, luminance-preserving saturation, reference white
times the brightness gain, ST 2084 encode, and the same limited-range
encode PQ bitmaps get. Settings are read when the menu is drawn, so a
menu on screen follows them on the next frame.

At 100% / 100% nothing is rerouted, so menus keep the GUI composite byte
for byte. Subtitles, the Kodi interface, SDR output (including CB1's SDR
compositor) and movie pixels are unchanged.

The idea comes from fandangos/Kodi-HDR-Edition's Android disc menu HDR
slider (GPL-2.0-or-later), re-done for the GBM GUI composite.

